<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Onogurs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Onogurs were a Turkic-speaking nomadic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a>, first recorded in 463 CE when they sent envoys to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Their name is usually interpreted as 'ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghurs">Oghurs</a>', meaning ten tribes. In the 7th century they formed, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>, and their name is thought to survive in the word 'Hungarian'.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Onogurs, also written Onoghurs or Oğurs, were Turkic nomadic horsemen who flourished on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region between the 5th and 7th centuries and spoke an Oghuric language. Their name is widely thought to derive from On-Oğur, ten Oğurs, meaning ten tribes. The Oghuric languages are sometimes called -r Turkic because certain words end in r where Common Turkic has z. Some scholars consider Chuvash the sole living representative of the Volga Bulgar language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a>, in 463 representatives of the Saraghurs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghurs">Oghurs</a> and Onoghurs came to the emperor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and explained that the Sabirs, who had been attacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a>, had driven them from their homeland. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jordanes">Jordanes</a>, in 551, noted the Hunuguri for their marten-skin trade, which suggests that they lived near forests and had contact with Finno-Ugrian peoples. In the 6th and 7th centuries the sources mention them mostly in connection with the Avar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürk</a> conquests.</p><p>Scholars are unsure how the union of Onoghurs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> formed, imagining a long process in which several groups merged, with the Onoghurs as one of the core tribes. Around 670 the Onoghurs arrived in the Avar Khaganate in the Carpathian Basin. In the mid-7th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a> organised the Onogurs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>, called by Byzantine writers the state of the Onoghundur-Bulgars; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> remarked in the mid-10th century that the Bulgars had formerly called themselves Onogundurs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Onoghur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> who settled on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in the 7th century converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 922 during the mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-fadlan">Ahmad ibn Fadlan</a> and lived in the present-day territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>. After the invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> in 1223-1236 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> annexed Volga Bulgaria, and the Onoghur-Bulgar group as a whole accepted the exonym <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>. The name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> is usually believed to derive from On-Oğur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Onogurs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 630s Kubrat of the Dulo clan threw off the overlordship of the Avars and united the <b>Onogur</b> and Bulgar tribes of the steppe between the Kuban and the lower Dnieper.</blockquote><a href="/state/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a></li></ul></section>
